ut how can i know the diff between versions - it can be image or another changes

helm history only shows metadata. To see the actual differences—image, memory, replicas, probes, environment variables—compare the rendered manifests of two revisions.

Compare revisions 2 and 3

Export both manifests:

helm get manifest medical-api -n medical-helm --revision 2 |
  Out-File revision-2.yaml -Encoding utf8

helm get manifest medical-api -n medical-helm --revision 3 |
  Out-File revision-3.yaml -Encoding utf8

Compare them with Git:

git diff --no-index revision-2.yaml revision-3.yaml

You should see something like:

resources:
  limits:
-   memory: 256Mi
+   memory: 300Mi

If the image changed, it would show:

- image: medical-api:1.0.1
+ image: medical-api:1.0.2

git diff --no-index may return exit code 1; that normally means differences were found, not that the comparison failed.

Compare Helm values

You can also inspect the values saved with each release:

helm get values medical-api -n medical-helm --revision 2 --all
helm get values medical-api -n medical-helm --revision 3 --all

The distinction:

helm get values — configuration supplied to Helm.
helm get manifest — final Kubernetes YAML generated from the chart and values.
helm history — revision number, status, chart version, and description.

For troubleshooting, comparing manifests is usually most useful because it shows exactly what Kubernetes received.

In a real GitOps project, Git gives us this comparison automatically:

git diff
git log --oneline
git show <commit>

That is one major benefit of Argo CD: every desired-state change has a Git commit showing exactly what changed.

////////////////////////////Mote //////


so in case there new image deployed ,should i change the yamls ?

Yes—but change the image tag in values.yaml, not in deployment.yaml.

For a new image:

image:
  repository: medical-api
  tag: "1.0.2"
  pullPolicy: Never

The Deployment template remains unchanged:

image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"

For local Kubernetes:

docker build -t medical-api:1.0.2 .
helm upgrade medical-api .\helm\medical-api -n medical-helm --wait

Kubernetes sees:

- image: medical-api:1.0.1
+ image: medical-api:1.0.2

and performs a rolling update.

You can also update Chart.yaml:

appVersion: "1.0.2"

That value is mainly descriptive; values.yaml → image.tag controls which image actually runs.

In our future GitOps flow:

CI builds medical-api:1.0.2
→ pushes it to Azure ACR
→ updates image.tag in values.yaml
→ commits the change
→ Argo CD detects it
→ AKS performs rolling deployment

Always use a new immutable tag. Reusing 1.0.1 or latest makes it difficult to identify changes and may not trigger a new deployment.